abstract: >
  TRIVORTEX is the working title of a research document that studies the
  three-body (and N-body) problem through a vortex formulation with a
  Chaplygin topological integral C_Ch = r^2*(theta_dot - q*A_theta). The
  executable document (code/trivortex_core*.py, ~2400 lines in each of three
  language mirrors) builds an LxL Hofstadter Hamiltonian with N embedded
  vortices, constructs a Lagrange-type closed-form rotating solution
  (Theorem 3.1: r_k(t) = sqrt(C_Ch)*(1 + eps*cos(omega*t + 2*pi*k/3)),
  theta_k(t) = omega*t + 2*pi*k/3, omega = (2*pi/T)*exp(C_Ch/pi)), and couples
  it to spectral statistics (GUE/GOE/Poisson), quantum-topological observables
  (Berry phase, Chern numbers, TKNN conductance, Dirac cones), chaos
  diagnostics (Lyapunov exponents, permutation tests) and real astronomical
  presets (Sun-Earth-Moon, Alpha Centauri, Pluto-Charon-Nix), with an
  interactive 15-mode menu and a seven-format report engine. An independent
  verification ladder (verification/trivortex/python/verify.py) re-derives the
  core claims from scratch and certifies: the exact 2*pi/3 equilateral
  choreography and periodicity of the closed form (V1, residuals <= 1e-12),
  the rigid Lagrange rotation at omega = 3*Gamma/(2*pi*a^2) (V2), and the
  conservation of the vortex integrals H, P, Q, I for equal and unequal
  circulations (V3-V4, drift <= 1e-10); the endpoint drift of the Chaplygin
  combination is recorded as a window-dependent diagnostic. A pytest guard
  pins the analytic reference values; seven Docker toolchains carry a staged
  multi-language verification roadmap (Coq, Lean 4, Rust, Isabelle-HOL, Agda,
  C++, Haskell). Formerly released as "AB-Cloud Vortex System for N-Body
  Problem"; the physics and the 22-section structure are unchanged.
